  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1121 PM CDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\nOn Friday, the ridge keeping our weather so quiet will slip off to \nthe east and a shortwave will quickly ripple in behind it. While \nthe dynamics aren't terribly impressive, I do agree with the \nprevious forecaster that there will be at least a marginal threat \nfor strong winds. Soundings show a dry boundary layer with a \naround a 20 degree dew point depression and decent CAPE, including\nDCAPE. Lapse rates and shear are both on the marginal side, but \nagain, could be just enough to help pose a damaging wind threat. \nAs of now, timing looks to be in the Friday evening/night range, \nso keep this in mind if you have any Friday night plans. The \nremainder of the weekend will feature at least low afternoon \nrain/storm chances, around 20%, with increasing tropospheric \nmoisture. We will be on the cusp of a very quick moving ridge, \nleaving us with plenty of afternoon instability, but little to no \ndynamic support. However, that will change come Monday, which\nleads me to...\n\nNext Monday, a negatively-tilted trough will push a strong cold \nfront through the area, bringing the chance for a more organized \nthreat for severe weather. Ahead of this low, we will be majorly \nwarm-sectored as increasing southerly winds stream in abundant \nmoisture with dew points slated to rise into the 60s and ambient \ntemperatures in the 80s. A strengthening low-level jet will bring \nplenty of shear into the picture with modest low-level lapse rates\nand daytime heating will result in plenty of instability. All to \nsay, the current setup favors an all-hazards severe threat for \nMonday, but there is still a lot of time between now and then for \nthings to change. The most important thing you can do now is stay \ntuned for updates as the forecast evolves. We have had a pretty \nquiet spring, and it's easy to forget we are in our peak severe \nweather season. We can't let our guards down just yet, though, so \nplease be sure to stay tuned for updates throughout the week.\n\nOne last thing to mention: rain! All of Middle TN is in at least \na moderate drought with a significant portion in a severe drought.\nWe can at least look forward to some much-needed rainfall with \nthe upcoming unsettled pattern. The increase in moisture will \nplace PWAT values above the 90th percentile climatologically, so \nepisodes of heavy rainfall will be possible.",
